  "client_view_text": " A process and a thread are two fundamental concepts in operating systems that are often confused with each other. A process is a program in execution, while a thread is a subset of the process that can execute instructions independently.\nA process is a program in execution, which includes its own memory space, open files, and system resources. Each process has its own program counter, stack, and local variables. When a process is created, the operating system allocates a new memory space for it, and the process runs independently of other processes.\nOn the other hand, a thread is a subset of a process. It is a flow of execution within a process. Threads share the same memory space as the process, and they can access the same variables and resources. Threads are lightweight and efficient, as they do not require a separate memory space or system resources.\nHere are some key differences between processes and threads:\nProcess Thread\n1. A process is a program in execution, while a thread is a subset of a process. 1. A thread is a flow of execution within a process.\n2. Each process has its own memory space, while threads share the same memory space. 2. Threads share the same memory space as the process.\n3. Processes are heavyweight, requiring a separate"
